---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Notes on the Next Ten Years"
  description="How Dinesh Pai thinks about businesses, supply and demand, patient capital, and where he sees opportunities in India over the next decade."
  maxWidth="max-w-5xl"
  showProgress={true}
>
  <div class="space-y-14 py-4 font-serif">

    <!-- BACK TO HOME LINK -->
    <div class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider">
      <a href="/" class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 hover:text-brand-light dark:hover:text-brand-dark hover:underline inline-flex items-center gap-1">
        &larr; Back to Home
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- TITLE & OVERVIEW -->
    <header class="space-y-4">
      <h1 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 !mt-0 !mb-2 leading-tight">
        Notes on the Next Ten Years
      </h1>
      <p class="font-serif text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
        Before getting into specific sectors, I want to share how I look at businesses and why some of them become good investment opportunities. That lens shapes where I see the biggest opportunities in India over the next decade.
      </p>

      <!-- Prominent Section Index -->
      <nav aria-label="Thesis sections" class="mt-5 p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-md border border-stone-300/80 dark:border-stone-700 bg-white/60 dark:bg-stone-900/50 space-y-3">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between border-b border-stone-200/80 dark:border-stone-800 pb-2 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider">
          <span class="font-bold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">On This Page</span>
          <span class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">6 Sections</span>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-2.5 font-mono text-xs">
          <a href="#first-principles" class="group flex items-baseline gap-2.5 p-2.5 rounded border border-stone-200/80 dark:border-stone-800 bg-white/70 dark:bg-stone-900/60 hover:border-brand-light dark:hover:border-brand-dark transition-colors">
            <span class="font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark">01</span>
            <span class="font-medium text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 group-hover:text-brand-light dark:group-hover:text-brand-dark">First Principles</span>
          </a>
          <a href="#income-slabs" class="group flex items-baseline gap-2.5 p-2.5 rounded border border-stone-200/80 dark:border-stone-800 bg-white/70 dark:bg-stone-900/60 hover:border-brand-light dark:hover:border-brand-dark transition-colors">
            <span class="font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark">02</span>
            <span class="font-medium text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 group-hover:text-brand-light dark:group-hover:text-brand-dark">Household Incomes</span>
          </a>
          <a href="#tax-filings" class="group flex items-baseline gap-2.5 p-2.5 rounded border border-stone-200/80 dark:border-stone-800 bg-white/70 dark:bg-stone-900/60 hover:border-brand-light dark:hover:border-brand-dark transition-colors">
            <span class="font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark">03</span>
            <span class="font-medium text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 group-hover:text-brand-light dark:group-hover:text-brand-dark">Tax Filings</span>
          </a>
          <a href="#what-gets-scarce" class="group flex items-baseline gap-2.5 p-2.5 rounded border border-stone-200/80 dark:border-stone-800 bg-white/70 dark:bg-stone-900/60 hover:border-brand-light dark:hover:border-brand-dark transition-colors">
            <span class="font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark">04</span>
            <span class="font-medium text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 group-hover:text-brand-light dark:group-hover:text-brand-dark">Consumer Scarcity</span>
          </a>
          <a href="#import-dependence" class="group flex items-baseline gap-2.5 p-2.5 rounded border border-stone-200/80 dark:border-stone-800 bg-white/70 dark:bg-stone-900/60 hover:border-brand-light dark:hover:border-brand-dark transition-colors">
            <span class="font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark">05</span>
            <span class="font-medium text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 group-hover:text-brand-light dark:group-hover:text-brand-dark">What India Imports Today</span>
          </a>
          <a href="#sovereign-infra" class="group flex items-baseline gap-2.5 p-2.5 rounded border border-stone-200/80 dark:border-stone-800 bg-white/70 dark:bg-stone-900/60 hover:border-brand-light dark:hover:border-brand-dark transition-colors">
            <span class="font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark">06</span>
            <span class="font-medium text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 group-hover:text-brand-light dark:group-hover:text-brand-dark">Industrial &amp; Sovereign Tech</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <!-- 01 / FIRST PRINCIPLES -->
    <section id="first-principles" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-5">
      <div class="border-b border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 pb-1.5 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline justify-between gap-1">
        <h2 class="font-mono text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">
          01 / First Principles
        </h2>
        <span class="text-xs text-stone-400 dark:text-stone-500 font-mono">
          Businesses &bull; Supply &amp; demand &bull; Capital
        </span>
      </div>

      <div class="text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base space-y-4">
        <h3 class="font-serif text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50 pt-1">
          Why does a business exist?
        </h3>
        <p>
          At its core, a business solves a problem that a customer is willing to pay for, and does it at a cost lower than what the customer pays. Fundraising, valuations, and brand campaigns all come later.
        </p>
        <p>
          Whether a business stays profitable over time comes down to supply and demand. If ten other people can set up the same supply in six months, competition will eat your margins. You see this all the time with thin software tools and consumer brands that use the same contract manufacturers. Everyone ends up spending heavily on marketing just to stay visible.
        </p>
        <p>
          Good businesses sit where demand is growing, but adding new supply is hard. You rarely find these gaps in macro reports. You find them by picking a specific niche and going deep into how the product is made, why customers are unhappy with current options, and what stops a competitor from copying it quickly.
        </p>

        <h3 class="font-serif text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50 pt-2">
          What makes it an investment opportunity?
        </h3>
        <p>
          A good business is not always a good venture investment. Many good businesses do not need outside equity at all. They can grow comfortably from their own customer cash flows.
        </p>
        <p>
          An investment makes sense when adding capital unlocks an outcome that would not happen otherwise. Maybe a founder needs capital to set up a factory, spend three years on R&amp;D before revenue starts, or build a rural supply chain.
        </p>
        <p>
          The kind of capital matters just as much as the amount. If a problem takes eight years of engineering or supply chain work to solve, it cannot be built with money that needs a quick markup in two years. Patient capital lets a team look at a niche, figure out what kind of time and effort the problem actually needs, and build at the right pace.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Summary Table: First-Principles Lens -->
      <figure class="data-card">
        <div class="card-header">
          <div>
            <h3 class="card-title">How I Look at a Business</h3>
            <p class="card-subtitle">Three questions before looking at projections</p>
          </div>
          <span class="card-badge">First Principles</span>
        </div>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Layer</th>
                <th>Question</th>
                <th>What I Look For</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">1. The Business</th>
                <td class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Why does it exist?</td>
                <td>A clear problem customers will pay for without discounts.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">2. The Niche</th>
                <td class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How hard is supply?</td>
                <td>Growing demand where new supply takes years of work or trust to build.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">3. The Capital</th>
                <td class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Why raise money?</td>
                <td>Capital goes into capacity, R&amp;D, or distribution that creates real impact.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <!-- 02 / HOUSEHOLD INCOMES -->
    <section id="income-slabs" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-5">
      <div class="border-b border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 pb-1.5 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline justify-between gap-1">
        <h2 class="font-mono text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">
          02 / Household Incomes
        </h2>
        <span class="text-xs text-stone-400 dark:text-stone-500 font-mono">
          PRICE ICE 360&deg; Survey &bull; 2020-21 vs. 2030-31E
        </span>
      </div>

      <div class="text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base space-y-4">
        <p>
          When I look at India over the next decade, the biggest shift is in household incomes.
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2020-21, India had about 301 million households. Roughly two-thirds earned less than ₹5 lakh a year. By 2030-31, the bottom two brackets will shrink in absolute terms. Around <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">74 million households will move into the middle class (₹5 to ₹30 lakh)</strong>, and another <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">24 million will cross ₹30 lakh</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Table 1: Household Income Slabs -->
      <figure class="data-card">
        <div class="card-header">
          <div>
            <h3 class="card-title">Indian Household Income Slabs</h3>
            <p class="card-subtitle">Constant 2020-21 prices &bull; Source: PRICE ICE 360&deg; Survey</p>
          </div>
          <span class="card-badge">301M &rarr; 358M Households</span>
        </div>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Income Group</th>
                <th>Annual Income</th>
                <th class="num">2020-21</th>
                <th class="num">2030-31E</th>
                <th class="num">Share (21 &rarr; 31E)</th>
                <th class="num">10Y Change</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Destitute</th>
                <td class="mono">&lt; ₹1.25 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num">45M</td>
                <td class="num">20M</td>
                <td class="num">14% &rarr; 5%</td>
                <td class="num text-stone-500">-56%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Aspirers</th>
                <td class="mono">₹1.25L - ₹5 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num">154M</td>
                <td class="num">138M</td>
                <td class="num">52% &rarr; 37%</td>
                <td class="num text-stone-500">-10%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Middle Class</th>
                <td class="mono">₹5L - ₹30 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">91M</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">165M</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">31% &rarr; 47%</td>
                <td class="num pos">+81%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Rich / Affluent</th>
                <td class="mono">&gt; ₹30 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">11M</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">35M</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">4% &rarr; 11%</td>
                <td class="num pos">+218%</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
            <tfoot>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Total India</th>
                <td class="mono">All Households</td>
                <td class="num">301M</td>
                <td class="num">358M</td>
                <td class="num">100% (1.42B &rarr; 1.53B)</td>
                <td class="num">+19%</td>
              </tr>
            </tfoot>
          </table>
        </div>

        <!-- Side-by-Side Pyramid to Diamond Visual -->
        <div class="mt-6 pt-5 border-t border-stone-200/90 dark:border-stone-800 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <!-- 2020-21 Pyramid -->
          <div class="p-4 rounded border border-stone-200/80 dark:border-stone-800 bg-white/40 dark:bg-stone-900/30 space-y-3">
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between border-b border-stone-200/70 dark:border-stone-800 pb-2">
              <span class="font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">2020-21: Bottom-Heavy</span>
              <span class="font-mono text-[11px] text-stone-500">66% Below ₹5L</span>
            </div>
            <div class="space-y-2.5 font-mono text-xs">
              <div class="grid grid-cols-[110px_1fr_52px] items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Rich (&gt;₹30L)</span>
                <div class="h-3 bg-stone-200/70 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-sm overflow-hidden">
                  <div class="h-full bg-stone-500" style="width: 7%"></div>
                </div>
                <span class="text-right text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">4%</span>
              </div>
              <div class="grid grid-cols-[110px_1fr_52px] items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Middle (₹5-30L)</span>
                <div class="h-3 bg-stone-200/70 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-sm overflow-hidden">
                  <div class="h-full bg-stone-500" style="width: 56%"></div>
                </div>
                <span class="text-right text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">31%</span>
              </div>
              <div class="grid grid-cols-[110px_1fr_52px] items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Aspirers (₹1.25-5L)</span>
                <div class="h-3 bg-stone-200/70 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-sm overflow-hidden">
                  <div class="h-full bg-stone-400 dark:bg-stone-600" style="width: 95%"></div>
                </div>
                <span class="text-right text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">52%</span>
              </div>
              <div class="grid grid-cols-[110px_1fr_52px] items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Destitute (&lt;₹1.25L)</span>
                <div class="h-3 bg-stone-200/70 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-sm overflow-hidden">
                  <div class="h-full bg-stone-400 dark:bg-stone-600" style="width: 26%"></div>
                </div>
                <span class="text-right text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">14%</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- 2030-31E Diamond -->
          <div class="p-4 rounded border border-brand-light/30 dark:border-brand-dark/30 bg-brand-light/[0.03] dark:bg-brand-dark/[0.04] space-y-3">
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between border-b border-stone-200/70 dark:border-stone-800 pb-2">
              <span class="font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark">2030-31E: Middle-Heavy</span>
              <span class="font-mono text-[11px] font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark">58% Above ₹5L</span>
            </div>
            <div class="space-y-2.5 font-mono text-xs">
              <div class="grid grid-cols-[110px_1fr_52px] items-center gap-2">
                <span class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Rich (&gt;₹30L)</span>
                <div class="h-3 bg-stone-200/70 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-sm overflow-hidden">
                  <div class="h-full bg-brand-light dark:bg-brand-dark" style="width: 21%"></div>
                </div>
                <span class="text-right font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark">11%</span>
              </div>
              <div class="grid grid-cols-[110px_1fr_52px] items-center gap-2">
                <span class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Middle (₹5-30L)</span>
                <div class="h-3 bg-stone-200/70 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-sm overflow-hidden">
                  <div class="h-full bg-brand-light dark:bg-brand-dark" style="width: 88%"></div>
                </div>
                <span class="text-right font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark">47%</span>
              </div>
              <div class="grid grid-cols-[110px_1fr_52px] items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Aspirers (₹1.25-5L)</span>
                <div class="h-3 bg-stone-200/70 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-sm overflow-hidden">
                  <div class="h-full bg-stone-400 dark:bg-stone-600" style="width: 68%"></div>
                </div>
                <span class="text-right text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">37%</span>
              </div>
              <div class="grid grid-cols-[110px_1fr_52px] items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Destitute (&lt;₹1.25L)</span>
                <div class="h-3 bg-stone-200/70 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-sm overflow-hidden">
                  <div class="h-full bg-stone-400 dark:bg-stone-600" style="width: 10%"></div>
                </div>
                <span class="text-right text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">5%</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <!-- 03 / TAX FILINGS -->
    <section id="tax-filings" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-5">
      <div class="border-b border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 pb-1.5 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline justify-between gap-1">
        <h2 class="font-mono text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">
          03 / Tax Filings
        </h2>
        <span class="text-xs text-stone-400 dark:text-stone-500 font-mono">
          CBDT &amp; e-Filing Portal &bull; FY21 vs. FY26
        </span>
      </div>

      <div class="text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base space-y-4">
        <p>
          Survey projections can be off, so I like checking them against income tax filings.
        </p>
        <p>
          Between FY21 and FY26, individual tax returns grew from <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">6.37 crore to 8.22 crore</strong>. Almost all of this growth came from higher brackets. Filings under ₹5 lakh dropped by 20%, while the <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">₹5 to ₹10 lakh bracket grew 81%</strong>. People declaring between <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">₹10 lakh and ₹50 lakh more than doubled to 1.50 crore</strong>, and filings <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">above ₹1 crore grew 128%</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Table 2: ITR Filings by Gross Total Income Slab -->
      <figure class="data-card">
        <div class="card-header">
          <div>
            <h3 class="card-title">Income Tax Filings by Slab</h3>
            <p class="card-subtitle">Individual ITRs filed &bull; Source: CBDT &amp; Income Tax Portal</p>
          </div>
          <span class="card-badge">6.37 Cr &rarr; 8.22 Cr Filers</span>
        </div>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Income Slab</th>
                <th class="num">FY21 Filers</th>
                <th class="num">FY26 Filers</th>
                <th class="num">Share (FY26)</th>
                <th class="num">5Y Change</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Up to ₹5 Lakh</th>
                <td class="num">3.47 Cr</td>
                <td class="num">2.76 Cr</td>
                <td class="num">33.6%</td>
                <td class="num text-stone-500">-20%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">₹5 Lakh - ₹10 Lakh</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">2.12 Cr</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">3.84 Cr</td>
                <td class="num">46.7%</td>
                <td class="num pos">+81%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">₹10 Lakh - ₹50 Lakh</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">72.2 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">1.50 Cr</td>
                <td class="num">18.3%</td>
                <td class="num pos">+108%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">₹50 Lakh - ₹1 Crore</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">3.95 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">7.64 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num">0.93%</td>
                <td class="num pos">+93%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Above ₹1 Crore</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">1.69 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">3.86 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num">0.47%</td>
                <td class="num pos">+128%</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
            <tfoot>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Total Returns</th>
                <td class="num">6.37 Cr</td>
                <td class="num">8.22 Cr</td>
                <td class="num">100.0%</td>
                <td class="num">+29%</td>
              </tr>
            </tfoot>
          </table>
        </div>

        <!-- Visual 5-Year ITR Slab Growth Bars -->
        <div class="mt-6 pt-5 border-t border-stone-200/90 dark:border-stone-800 space-y-3">
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
              5-Year Growth by Bracket
            </span>
            <span class="font-mono text-[11px] text-stone-500">Filers &gt;₹10L: 77.8L &rarr; 1.62 Cr (+108%)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-5 gap-3 pt-1 font-mono text-xs">
            <div class="p-3 rounded border border-stone-200/80 dark:border-stone-800 bg-white/40 dark:bg-stone-900/30">
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-500">Up to ₹5L</div>
              <div class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-500 mt-0.5">-20%</div>
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-400 mt-0.5">3.47Cr &rarr; 2.76Cr</div>
            </div>
            <div class="p-3 rounded border border-brand-light/25 dark:border-brand-dark/25 bg-brand-light/[0.03] dark:bg-brand-dark/[0.04]">
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">₹5L - ₹10L</div>
              <div class="text-lg font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark mt-0.5">+81%</div>
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-500 mt-0.5">2.12Cr &rarr; 3.84Cr</div>
            </div>
            <div class="p-3 rounded border border-brand-light/25 dark:border-brand-dark/25 bg-brand-light/[0.03] dark:bg-brand-dark/[0.04]">
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">₹10L - ₹50L</div>
              <div class="text-lg font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark mt-0.5">+108%</div>
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-500 mt-0.5">72.2L &rarr; 1.50Cr</div>
            </div>
            <div class="p-3 rounded border border-brand-light/25 dark:border-brand-dark/25 bg-brand-light/[0.03] dark:bg-brand-dark/[0.04]">
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">₹50L - ₹1Cr</div>
              <div class="text-lg font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark mt-0.5">+93%</div>
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-500 mt-0.5">3.95L &rarr; 7.64L</div>
            </div>
            <div class="p-3 rounded border border-brand-light/25 dark:border-brand-dark/25 bg-brand-light/[0.03] dark:bg-brand-dark/[0.04]">
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Above ₹1Cr</div>
              <div class="text-lg font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark mt-0.5">+128%</div>
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-500 mt-0.5">1.69L &rarr; 3.86L</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="card-footnote">
          About 1.62 crore Indians now declare over ₹10 lakh a year, double the number from five years ago. At the same time, that is still just over 1% of our population. The base is growing fast, and it is still early.
        </p>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <!-- 04 / CONSUMER SCARCITY -->
    <section id="what-gets-scarce" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-5">
      <div class="border-b border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 pb-1.5 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline justify-between gap-1">
        <h2 class="font-mono text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">
          04 / Consumer Scarcity
        </h2>
        <span class="text-xs text-stone-400 dark:text-stone-500 font-mono">
          Rising incomes &bull; Tight physical supply
        </span>
      </div>

      <div class="text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base space-y-4">
        <p>
          Once a family crosses ₹10 lakh in annual income, basic needs like food, clothes, and mobile data are sorted. Extra income goes toward a better home, travel, health, clean food, and managing savings.
        </p>
        <p>
          When millions of families want these things at the same time, physical supply struggles to keep up. You cannot build a good hotel, a clean dairy supply chain, or a hospital overnight. Japan in the 1970s, South Korea in the late 1980s, and China after 2008 all went through this same phase after crossing $3,000 in per-capita GDP.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Table 3: Historical Asian Consumption Inflections -->
      <figure class="data-card">
        <div class="card-header">
          <div>
            <h3 class="card-title">What Changes Past $3,000 Per-Capita GDP</h3>
            <p class="card-subtitle">Historical shifts in East Asia vs. India</p>
          </div>
          <span class="card-badge">Asian Precedent</span>
        </div>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Country</th>
                <th>Period</th>
                <th class="num">GDP / Capita</th>
                <th>Where Spending Grew Fastest</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Japan</th>
                <td class="mono">1972 - 1980</td>
                <td class="num">$2,900 &rarr; $9,400</td>
                <td>Urban housing, rail travel, home appliances (ACs, refrigerators)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">South Korea</th>
                <td class="mono">1987 - 1996</td>
                <td class="num">$3,500 &rarr; $13,000</td>
                <td>Apartments, leisure &amp; hotels, electronics, cars</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">China</th>
                <td class="mono">2008 - 2017</td>
                <td class="num">$3,400 &rarr; $8,800</td>
                <td>Domestic flights &amp; hotels, diagnostics, protein &amp; dairy, cooling</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">India</th>
                <td class="mono">2025 - 2035E</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">$2,900 &rarr; $6,500+</td>
                <td>Housing, travel, clean food, wealth management, cooling &amp; medtech</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <div class="text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base space-y-4">
        <p>
          Five areas where I see demand running ahead of supply:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-3 pl-5 list-disc mt-2">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">Housing:</strong> Families moving up from basic apartments want well-run communities with reliable water, power, and open spaces. Good urban land with clean titles and credible builders is hard to find.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">Travel and Leisure:</strong> India has built airports and highways faster than good places to stay. There is still a big shortage of clean, mid-priced hotels and holiday spots within driving distance of large cities.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">Clean Food:</strong> Rising incomes have come alongside high rates of diabetes and metabolic illness. More families now read labels and want clean staples. The hard part here is not branding or 10-minute delivery. It is farm sourcing, soil health, and cold chains.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">Financial Services:</strong> As more people move savings from gold and fixed deposits into financial markets, they need simple tools and honest advice to manage their money.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">Cooling and Healthcare Hardware:</strong> Less than 10% of Indian homes have an AC today, compared to over 60% in China. As summers get hotter and incomes rise, demand for efficient cooling, motors, and airflow hardware will keep growing. On healthcare, as more parents age in Tier-2 and Tier-3 towns, India needs affordable diagnostics and out-of-hospital recovery care.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 05 / WHAT INDIA IMPORTS TODAY -->
    <section id="import-dependence" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-5">
      <div class="border-b border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 pb-1.5 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline justify-between gap-1">
        <h2 class="font-mono text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">
          05 / What India Imports Today
        </h2>
        <span class="text-xs text-stone-400 dark:text-stone-500 font-mono">
          Ministry of Mines &bull; CSEP &bull; GTRI &bull; IMTMA
        </span>
      </div>

      <div class="text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base space-y-4">
        <p>
          Rising incomes tell you what Indian households will buy more of. Trade data tells you where our factories and infrastructure are still fragile.
        </p>
        <p>
          Over the last decade, India has set up assembly lines for phones, EVs, solar panels, and medicines. That is real progress. Still, if you look one layer deeper at the metals, chemicals, magnets, and machines that feed those factories, India imports almost everything from one or two countries.
        </p>
        <p>
          For years, buying from the cheapest global supplier worked fine. Today, countries use export controls on minerals, magnets, and factory tools as leverage. If access is tightened tomorrow, several large sectors in India will struggle to keep running.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Table: Verified Import Reliance Across Critical Inputs -->
      <figure class="data-card">
        <div class="card-header">
          <div>
            <h3 class="card-title">Critical Imports in India's Supply Chain</h3>
            <p class="card-subtitle">Share of domestic demand met by imports &bull; Source: Ministry of Mines, CSEP, GTRI &amp; IMTMA</p>
          </div>
          <span class="card-badge">65% to 100% Imported</span>
        </div>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Material / Input</th>
                <th class="num">Import Share</th>
                <th>Main Suppliers</th>
                <th>What Stops If Cut Off</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Lithium, Cobalt &amp; Nickel</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">100%</td>
                <td>China (refining), Chile, Australia</td>
                <td>EV batteries, grid storage, electronics</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Rare-Earth Magnets (NdFeB)</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">&gt;90%</td>
                <td>China (~90% global processing)</td>
                <td>EV traction motors, wind turbines, drones</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Solar Wafers &amp; Polysilicon</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">&gt;90%</td>
                <td>China</td>
                <td>Domestic solar cell and panel plants</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">CRGO Electrical Steel</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">~90%</td>
                <td>China, Japan, South Korea, Russia</td>
                <td>Power grid and distribution transformers</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Potash &amp; Phosphate Inputs</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">85-100%</td>
                <td>Canada, Russia, Jordan, Morocco</td>
                <td>Fertilizers and farm productivity</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Pharma KSMs &amp; Bulk APIs</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">65-70%</td>
                <td>China (80-90% in antibiotics)</td>
                <td>Basic medicines, antibiotics, paracetamol</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">High-Precision CNC Machines</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">60-65%</td>
                <td>Germany, Japan, Taiwan, China</td>
                <td>Auto, aerospace, and electronics factories</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <!-- Visual Breakdown of Core Industrial Chokepoints -->
        <div class="mt-6 pt-5 border-t border-stone-200/90 dark:border-stone-800 space-y-3">
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
              Where the Bottlenecks Sit
            </span>
            <span class="font-mono text-[11px] text-stone-500">Local assembly vs. imported core inputs</span>
          </div>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-3 pt-1 font-mono text-xs">
            <div class="p-3 rounded border border-brand-light/25 dark:border-brand-dark/25 bg-brand-light/[0.03] dark:bg-brand-dark/[0.04]">
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Battery Minerals</div>
              <div class="text-lg font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark mt-0.5">100%</div>
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-500 mt-0.5">Li, Co, Ni imported</div>
            </div>
            <div class="p-3 rounded border border-brand-light/25 dark:border-brand-dark/25 bg-brand-light/[0.03] dark:bg-brand-dark/[0.04]">
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Magnets &amp; Wafers</div>
              <div class="text-lg font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark mt-0.5">&gt;90%</div>
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-500 mt-0.5">Single-country risk</div>
            </div>
            <div class="p-3 rounded border border-brand-light/25 dark:border-brand-dark/25 bg-brand-light/[0.03] dark:bg-brand-dark/[0.04]">
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Transformer Steel</div>
              <div class="text-lg font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark mt-0.5">~90%</div>
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-500 mt-0.5">4L of 4.5L tonnes/yr</div>
            </div>
            <div class="p-3 rounded border border-brand-light/25 dark:border-brand-dark/25 bg-brand-light/[0.03] dark:bg-brand-dark/[0.04]">
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Fermentation APIs</div>
              <div class="text-lg font-bold text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark mt-0.5">80-90%</div>
              <div class="text-[11px] text-stone-500 mt-0.5">Penicillins &amp; KSMs</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="card-footnote">
          Take power transformers as one example. India needs about 4.0 to 4.5 lakh tonnes of cold-rolled grain-oriented (CRGO) steel every year to build transformer cores. Domestic mills produce less than 50,000 tonnes. The rest has to be imported.
        </p>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <!-- 06 / INDUSTRIAL & SOVEREIGN TECH -->
    <section id="sovereign-infra" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-5">
      <div class="border-b border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 pb-1.5 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline justify-between gap-1">
        <h2 class="font-mono text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">
          06 / Industrial &amp; Sovereign Tech
        </h2>
        <span class="text-xs text-stone-400 dark:text-stone-500 font-mono">
          Power grid &bull; Factory machines &bull; Metals
        </span>
      </div>

      <div class="text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base space-y-4">
        <p>
          That import table points straight to some of the most important industrial opportunities in India over the next decade. A country of 1.4 billion people cannot rely entirely on imports for its power grid, factory machines, critical materials, or defense.
        </p>
        <p>
          You cannot fix these gaps with a software wrapper or a quick trading business. Solving them takes five to eight years of metallurgy, hardware engineering, and factory testing before large revenues show up. That is why patient capital fits naturally here.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Table 5: Sovereign Industrial Bottlenecks -->
      <figure class="data-card">
        <div class="card-header">
          <div>
            <h3 class="card-title">Where Domestic Capacity Is Needed</h3>
            <p class="card-subtitle">Replacing critical imports across India's industrial stack</p>
          </div>
          <span class="card-badge">Theme 2</span>
        </div>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Layer</th>
                <th>Bottleneck</th>
                <th class="num">Development Time</th>
                <th>What Needs to Be Built</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Power Grid</th>
                <td>HV Transformers &amp; Storage</td>
                <td class="num">20-30 mo wait</td>
                <td>Grid storage, transformers, fire-safety materials, commercial clean power</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Metals &amp; Motors</th>
                <td>Rare-Earths &amp; Electrical Steel</td>
                <td class="num">4-7 yr R&amp;D</td>
                <td>Rare-earth-free motors, mineral refining, battery recycling</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Factory Tools</th>
                <td>CNCs, Spindles &amp; Test Rigs</td>
                <td class="num">5-8 yr R&amp;D</td>
                <td>Multi-axis CNCs, actuators, inspection systems, industrial robotics</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Space &amp; Defense</th>
                <td>Satellites &amp; Propulsion</td>
                <td class="num">5-8 yr R&amp;D</td>
                <td>Earth observation satellites, solid propulsion, navigation sensors</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <div class="text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base space-y-4">
        <ul class="space-y-3 pl-5 list-disc mt-2">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">The Power Grid:</strong> Setting up solar plants is no longer the main problem. Moving and storing that power is. As EVs, ACs, factories, and data centers grow, India needs large battery storage, transformers, fire-safety materials, and better ways for factories to buy clean power.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">Engineering Around Missing Materials:</strong> When a country does not have local deposits of rare earths or lithium, there are two choices. One is to build refining and recycling capacity at home. The other is to engineer products that do not need the imported material at all, such as high-efficiency motors built without rare-earth magnets.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">Factory Machines:</strong> Walk through an Indian factory making electronics or auto parts today, and almost all the CNC machines, spindles, and testing rigs are imported. Building precision machines and factory tools in India is a multi-decade opportunity.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">Space and Defense:</strong> In strategic sectors, imported hardware can be cut off when you need it most. India needs domestic teams building satellites, sensors, and rocket propulsion from scratch.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p class="pt-2">
          If you are building or thinking about any of these areas, I would love to chat. Reach out on <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/paidinesh1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark font-bold hover:underline">LinkedIn</a> or <a href="https://x.com/paidinesh7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark font-bold hover:underline">X</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

